insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('ci-f-069','cissp','core',1,'1.1',$$What are the four canons of the (ISC)² Code of Ethics, in order?$$,$$1) Protect society, the commonwealth, and the infrastructure. 2) Act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly, and legally. 3) Provide diligent and competent service to principals. 4) Advance and protect the profession. Canon 1 takes precedence when canons conflict.$$),
('ci-f-070','cissp','core',1,'1.2',$$What does the STRIDE threat modeling mnemonic stand for?$$,$$Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege - a Microsoft-originated model mapping threats to security properties they violate.$$),
('ci-f-071','cissp','core',1,'1.3',$$What are the four risk response strategies?$$,$$Avoid (eliminate the activity causing the risk), Mitigate (reduce likelihood or impact), Transfer (shift risk to a third party via insurance or outsourcing), and Accept (a documented, informed decision to retain the risk).$$),
('ci-f-072','cissp','core',1,'1.4',$$How do a BIA, BCP, and DRP differ?$$,$$A Business Impact Analysis identifies critical processes and their RTO/RPO; a Business Continuity Plan keeps critical business functions running during a disruption; a Disaster Recovery Plan restores IT systems and infrastructure after a disaster. The BIA's findings drive both plans.$$),
('ci-f-073','cissp','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the difference between due diligence and due care?$$,$$Due diligence is the research and investigation done before acting - knowing what should be done. Due care is taking the reasonable, prudent actions based on that knowledge - actually doing it.$$),
('ci-f-074','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What are the three broad categories of law relevant to CISSP?$$,$$Criminal law (state vs. individual, can result in imprisonment), civil/tort law (compensates a wronged party, typically monetary), and administrative/regulatory law (agency-enforced rules, e.g., HIPAA enforcement actions).$$),
('ci-f-075','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What are the four types of intellectual property protection?$$,$$Patent (protects an invention for ~20 years), copyright (protects the expression of an idea, life of author + 70 years), trademark (protects brand identifiers like logos/names), and trade secret (protects confidential business information indefinitely, as long as it stays secret).$$),
('ci-f-076','cissp','core',1,'1.3',$$What are the six functional types of security controls?$$,$$Preventive (stops an incident), Detective (identifies one occurring), Corrective (limits damage/restores after), Deterrent (discourages an attempt), Compensating (alternative when the primary control is not feasible), and Directive (policy/procedure guiding behavior).$$),
('ci-f-077','cissp','core',1,'1.4',$$Why must third-party risk management be an ongoing process rather than a one-time gate?$$,$$A vendor's security posture, ownership, subcontractors, and breach history can change after onboarding - periodic reassessment (and a right-to-audit clause in the contract) catches drift that a single point-in-time review would miss.$$),
('ci-f-078','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between a data owner and a data custodian?$$,$$The data owner (typically a business executive) is accountable for classification and access decisions. The data custodian implements the day-to-day technical controls (backups, encryption, access lists) the owner directs.$$),
('ci-f-079','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What are the stages of the data lifecycle?$$,$$Create, Store, Use, Share, Archive, and Destroy - security requirements and controls differ at each stage, from classification at creation through sanitization at destruction.$$),
('ci-f-080','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What is the difference between Clear, Purge, and Destroy in NIST SP 800-88 media sanitization?$$,$$Clear uses standard read/write commands (e.g., overwrite) protecting against simple, non-lab recovery, suitable for reuse within the same organization. Purge uses more advanced techniques (e.g., cryptographic erase, degaussing) resisting laboratory-level recovery. Destroy physically disintegrates the media, providing the highest assurance.$$),
('ci-f-081','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$What are the three states of data, and how is each typically protected?$$,$$Data at rest (protected by storage/disk encryption), data in transit (protected by TLS/IPsec), and data in use (protected by techniques like memory encryption or confidential computing while actively processed).$$),
('ci-f-082','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between scoping and tailoring a control baseline?$$,$$Scoping determines which controls from a full baseline actually apply to a given system (e.g., excluding controls only relevant to higher-impact systems). Tailoring then customizes the parameters of the applicable controls to fit the organization's specific environment and risk tolerance.$$),
('ci-f-083','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What are typical commercial data classification levels?$$,$$Public, Internal, Confidential, and Restricted (or similarly named tiers) - each tier maps to specific handling, access, and disclosure requirements, distinct from government tiers like Unclassified/Confidential/Secret/Top Secret.$$),
('ci-f-084','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What does the Bell-LaPadula security model enforce?$$,$$A confidentiality-focused model with a simple security property ("no read up" - cannot read data classified higher than one's clearance) and a star property ("no write down" - cannot write data to a lower classification level).$$),
('ci-f-085','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What does the Biba security model enforce?$$,$$An integrity-focused model with a simple integrity axiom ("no read down") and a star-integrity axiom ("no write up") - preventing lower-integrity subjects from corrupting higher-integrity data.$$),
('ci-f-086','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What does the Clark-Wilson model enforce?$$,$$Integrity through well-formed transactions (data can only be modified via a trusted, vetted program, never directly) and separation of duties (an access triple of subject-program-object, requiring distinct roles for related actions).$$),
('ci-f-087','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What does the Brewer-Nash (Chinese Wall) model enforce?$$,$$Dynamic access restrictions that change based on a subject's prior access history, specifically to prevent conflicts of interest - e.g., blocking a consultant from accessing a competitor's data once they've accessed a rival's.$$),
('ci-f-088','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What do Common Criteria Evaluation Assurance Levels (EAL1-EAL7) measure?$$,$$The rigor and depth of the evaluation/testing process a product underwent, not a guarantee of real-world security or the absence of vulnerabilities - a higher EAL means more thorough assurance testing, not automatically "more secure."$$),
('ci-f-089','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What three properties must a reference monitor satisfy?$$,$$Complete mediation (always invoked for every access), tamperproof (cannot be bypassed or altered), and small enough to be formally verifiable - foundational to trusted computing base design.$$),
('ci-f-090','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$When should symmetric vs. asymmetric cryptography be used?$$,$$Symmetric cryptography is fast and efficient for bulk data encryption. Asymmetric cryptography is used for key exchange, digital signatures, and scenarios requiring a securely shareable public key - most real systems use asymmetric crypto only to exchange a symmetric session key.$$),
('ci-f-091','cissp','core',3,'3.5',$$What are the CPTED principles for physical security design?$$,$$Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design includes natural surveillance (sightlines), natural access control (guiding movement through defined paths), and territorial reinforcement (clear ownership cues like signage/landscaping) to discourage illegitimate activity through design.$$),
('ci-f-092','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$What does each OSI layer do, at a high level?$$,$$7 Application (user-facing protocols), 6 Presentation (formatting/encryption), 5 Session (session management), 4 Transport (reliable delivery, TCP/UDP), 3 Network (logical addressing/routing), 2 Data Link (physical addressing/framing), 1 Physical (bits on the wire).$$),
('ci-f-093','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is the difference between IPsec Transport mode and Tunnel mode?$$,$$Transport mode encrypts only the payload, leaving the original IP header intact (host-to-host). Tunnel mode encrypts and encapsulates the entire original packet, including its header, inside a new packet (gateway-to-gateway VPNs).$$),
('ci-f-094','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is the difference between IPsec AH and ESP?$$,$$AH (Authentication Header) provides integrity and authentication only, no encryption. ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload) provides encryption plus integrity and authentication - ESP is used whenever confidentiality is required.$$),
('ci-f-095','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$How do packet-filtering, stateful, and next-generation firewalls differ?$$,$$Packet filtering evaluates each packet in isolation against static rules. Stateful firewalls track connection state, permitting return traffic for established sessions. Next-generation firewalls add application awareness, deep packet inspection, and integrated IPS/threat intelligence.$$),
('ci-f-096','cissp','core',4,'4.4',$$What is the difference between an IDS and an IPS?$$,$$An IDS is typically deployed out-of-band and can only detect and alert on suspicious activity. An IPS sits inline with traffic and can actively block malicious traffic in real time, preventing it from reaching its destination.$$),
('ci-f-097','cissp','core',4,'4.5',$$How does WPA3's SAE improve on WPA2's four-way handshake?$$,$$Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE) resists offline dictionary/brute-force attacks against a captured handshake, closing a class of weaknesses (exploited by attacks like KRACK) present in WPA2's four-way handshake.$$),
('ci-f-098','cissp','core',4,'4.6',$$Why is SRTP used instead of plain RTP for VoIP?$$,$$Plain RTP transmits voice media in the clear, allowing eavesdropping if captured on the network. SRTP encrypts the media stream, protecting call confidentiality.$$),
('ci-f-099','cissp','core',4,'4.7',$$What is the security purpose of network segmentation (e.g., via VLANs)?$$,$$Limits lateral movement and blast radius - if one segment (e.g., IoT devices or guest Wi-Fi) is compromised, segmentation with restrictive inter-segment routing prevents easy pivoting toward more sensitive segments like finance servers.$$),
('ci-f-100','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What do the four components of the IAAA model stand for?$$,$$Identification (claiming an identity), Authentication (proving that claim), Authorization (determining what the authenticated identity may do), and Accountability (tracing actions back to a specific identity via logs).$$),
('ci-f-101','cissp','core',5,'5.2',$$How do DAC, MAC, RBAC, and ABAC differ?$$,$$DAC: the resource owner decides access at their discretion. MAC: access is enforced by the system based on labels/clearances the user cannot alter. RBAC: permissions are tied to a job role. ABAC: access is evaluated dynamically based on multiple attributes (device, time, department, etc.) at request time.$$),
('ci-f-102','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What is the relationship between SAML, OAuth, and OIDC?$$,$$SAML is an XML-based standard for web SSO between an Identity Provider and Service Provider. OAuth 2.0 handles delegated authorization (granting limited access without sharing credentials). OpenID Connect (OIDC) adds an authentication layer on top of OAuth 2.0.$$),
('ci-f-103','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$Why is Kerberos sensitive to clock synchronization?$$,$$Kerberos tickets are time-stamped and typically rejected outside a small allowed clock skew (often ~5 minutes) specifically to prevent replay attacks - significant clock drift between a client and the KDC causes authentication failures.$$),
('ci-f-104','cissp','core',5,'5.5',$$What are the common multi-factor authentication factor categories?$$,$$Something you know (password/PIN), something you have (hardware token/smartphone), something you are (biometric), somewhere you are (geolocation), and something you do (behavioral biometrics).$$),
('ci-f-105','cissp','core',5,'5.6',$$Why is timely deprovisioning critical after employee termination?$$,$$Delayed account disablement creates an avoidable window in which a terminated employee (or anyone with their credentials) could access systems without authorization - timely, consistent offboarding closes this insider-threat risk window.$$),
('ci-f-106','cissp','core',5,'5.7',$$What is a pass-the-hash attack?$$,$$An attacker captures a cached password hash (e.g., NTLM) and reuses the hash itself to authenticate to other systems, without ever cracking the underlying plaintext password.$$),
('ci-f-107','cissp','core',5,'5.7',$$What is credential stuffing?$$,$$An attack using username/password pairs leaked from one breach against many unrelated sites, succeeding wherever users reused the same credentials across services.$$),
('ci-f-108','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What is the difference between black box, white box, and gray box penetration testing?$$,$$Black box: tester has no internal knowledge, simulating an external attacker. White box: tester has full knowledge (source code, diagrams), giving the most thorough coverage. Gray box: tester has partial knowledge (e.g., standard user credentials), a middle ground.$$),
('ci-f-109','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$How does a vulnerability assessment differ from a penetration test?$$,$$A vulnerability assessment identifies and catalogs potential weaknesses (e.g., via scanning) without proving exploitability. A penetration test actively attempts to exploit findings to demonstrate real-world impact.$$),
('ci-f-110','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What do the NIST SP 800-53A assessment methods Examine, Interview, and Test each involve?$$,$$Examine reviews documents/artifacts (e.g., a configuration standard). Interview gathers information from personnel (e.g., asking an admin about their process). Test directly exercises the mechanism (e.g., attempting to trigger an alert).$$),
('ci-f-111','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$What is the difference between a SOC 2 Type I and Type II report?$$,$$Type I assesses whether controls were suitably designed at a single point in time. Type II evaluates whether those controls actually operated effectively over a period (commonly 6-12 months) - providing stronger ongoing assurance.$$),
('ci-f-112','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What is a synthetic transaction?$$,$$An automated script that periodically simulates a real end-to-end user action (e.g., completing a test purchase) to proactively verify functionality and confirm monitoring/alerting would fire on a failure.$$),
('ci-f-113','cissp','core',6,'6.6',$$What is the difference between SAST and DAST?$$,$$SAST (Static Application Security Testing) analyzes source code without executing it, catching issues earlier in the SDLC. DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing) tests a running application with live inputs, catching runtime-only issues.$$),
('ci-f-114','cissp','core',6,'6.7',$$What is the difference between a KPI and a KRI?$$,$$A Key Performance Indicator measures how effectively an existing process is performing against a target (e.g., average patch time). A Key Risk Indicator provides forward-looking early warning of rising risk exposure (e.g., a trending increase in failed logins).$$),
('ci-f-115','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What is required to maintain a valid chain of custody for evidence?$$,$$An unbroken, documented trail recording who handled the evidence, when, and for what purpose at every step - gaps in this trail are a common basis for evidence being challenged or excluded in legal proceedings.$$),
('ci-f-116','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What are the (ISC)² incident management phases?$$,$$Detection, Response, Mitigation, Reporting, Recovery, Remediation, and Lessons Learned - progressing from identifying an incident through containing it, restoring operations, and improving future response.$$),
('ci-f-117','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$What is the difference between change management and configuration management?$$,$$Change management governs the structured request-review-approve-implement workflow for intentional modifications (often via a Change Advisory Board). Configuration management establishes and enforces a known-good baseline state, detecting unauthorized drift from it.$$),
('ci-f-118','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$Why should patches be tested before production deployment, even under time pressure?$$,$$An untested patch can itself cause outages or break critical functionality - validating in a representative lab environment first, even for urgent zero-day fixes, avoids trading one incident for another.$$),
('ci-f-119','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$What are the main types of BCP/DRP tests, from lowest to highest risk/realism?$$,$$Tabletop exercise (discussion only, no systems touched) → walkthrough → simulation test → parallel test (DR site runs alongside production) → full interruption test (production actually fails over, highest risk and highest realism).$$),
('ci-f-120','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$How do RAID 1 and RAID 5 differ?$$,$$RAID 1 mirrors identical data across drives with no parity math, tolerating full drive loss at 50% storage efficiency. RAID 5 stripes data with distributed parity across all drives, tolerating a single drive failure with better storage efficiency.$$),
('ci-f-121','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What is a mantrap and what does it prevent?$$,$$A small space with two interlocking doors where the second only unlocks after the first closes and the occupant is verified, allowing just one person through per authentication event - it directly prevents tailgating/piggybacking.$$),
('ci-f-122','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$What does DLP / egress monitoring do?$$,$$Inspects outbound traffic for sensitive data patterns (e.g., credit card numbers) and can automatically block or quarantine transmissions that violate policy, such as an employee emailing regulated data to a personal address.$$),
('ci-f-123','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What does "shift-left" security mean in the SDLC?$$,$$Integrating security testing (like SAST scanning) earlier in the development lifecycle - e.g., at every commit rather than only before release - since defects are dramatically cheaper to fix the earlier they are found.$$),
('ci-f-124','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What is the purpose of the OWASP Top 10?$$,$$A standardized, widely recognized awareness baseline listing the most critical and common web application security risks, used broadly for training and prioritization across the industry.$$),
('ci-f-125','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What is the difference between an aggregation attack and an inference attack?$$,$$Aggregation combines multiple individually low-sensitivity pieces of data to directly reveal something more sensitive. Inference deduces restricted information from accessible related data through reasoning/correlation, without directly combining data sources.$$),
('ci-f-126','cissp','core',8,'8.4',$$Why is rate limiting an important API security control?$$,$$Without it, a public API endpoint can be flooded by automated scripted requests, degrading service for all legitimate users - rate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a given period.$$),
('ci-f-127','cissp','core',8,'8.5',$$Why do secure code repositories require mandatory peer review and secrets scanning?$$,$$To catch accidental credential leaks (like a hardcoded API key) or security-relevant flaws before they are permanently committed into shared version-controlled history.$$),
('ci-f-128','cissp','core',8,'8.6',$$How do a worm and a trojan differ?$$,$$A worm self-propagates across a network autonomously, needing no user interaction or host file. A trojan disguises malicious functionality inside seemingly legitimate software, relying on the user to voluntarily install it.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('ci-q-056','cissp',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$An employee notices a close friend and coworker is violating a documented company security policy, but says nothing to avoid damaging the friendship. Which (ISC)² Code of Ethics canon does this failure to report most directly violate?$q$,$q$["Act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly, and legally","Advance and protect the profession","Provide diligent and competent service to principals","There is no ethical obligation to report a friend"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Staying silent to protect a personal relationship over doing what is right conflicts with acting honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly, and legally - the canon most directly tied to personal integrity in professional conduct.$q$),
('ci-q-057','cissp',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$An attacker intercepts a firmware update in transit and alters its contents before it reaches the device, without being detected by any integrity check. Which STRIDE category does this attack best represent?$q$,$q$["Tampering","Spoofing","Repudiation","Denial of service"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Modifying data or code in transit is the definition of Tampering in the STRIDE model - it violates integrity, distinct from spoofing (identity), repudiation (denying an action), or denial of service (availability).$q$),
('ci-q-058','cissp',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$A data center houses a server valued at $200,000. Historical data shows a fire causes a 60% loss of the asset's value, and such fires occur on average once every 20 years. What is the Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE)?$q$,$q$["$6,000","$120,000","$3,000","$40,000"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SLE = asset value x exposure factor = $200,000 x 0.6 = $120,000. ARO = 1 event every 20 years = 0.05. ALE = SLE x ARO = $120,000 x 0.05 = $6,000.$q$),
('ci-q-059','cissp',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$After implementing all cost-justified mitigating controls, a residual risk still exceeds the organization's documented risk appetite, and further technical mitigation is not economically feasible. What is the most appropriate next step?$q$,$q$["Formally transfer the remaining risk, such as through a cyber-insurance policy, or obtain documented executive acceptance","Ignore the residual risk since mitigation was already attempted","Delete the risk register entry since the risk was already addressed","Refuse to deploy any further mitigating controls ever again"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$When further mitigation is not cost-justified, the remaining options are to transfer the risk (e.g., insurance) or formally accept it at an appropriate level of authority - simply ignoring or discarding a residual risk above appetite is not defensible risk management.$q$),
('ci-q-060','cissp',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$Which activity's primary output is used to determine an organization's Recovery Time Objective (RTO) and Recovery Point Objective (RPO) for each critical business process?$q$,$q$["Business Impact Analysis (BIA)","Penetration test","Security awareness training","Vulnerability scan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The BIA analyzes the impact of an outage on each business process over time, and its output directly informs the RTO/RPO targets used to build the BCP and DRP.$q$),
('ci-q-061','cissp',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A company suffers a breach after having implemented no meaningful security controls despite years of known industry threats, and is later found liable in a civil lawsuit. Which concept most directly explains the basis for that liability?$q$,$q$["Failure to exercise due care","Failure to obtain a patent","Failure to purchase cyber insurance","Failure to conduct a penetration test specifically"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Due care is the standard of reasonably prudent action a similarly situated organization would take; a documented, sustained failure to implement any meaningful controls is a classic due care failure supporting civil liability.$q$),
('ci-q-062','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$A beverage company protects its proprietary flavor formula by keeping it confidential indefinitely rather than filing it with any government agency. Which form of intellectual property protection is this?$q$,$q$["Trade secret","Patent","Copyright","Trademark"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Trade secrets protect confidential information for as long as it remains undisclosed and reasonable protective measures are maintained - unlike a patent, no filing or expiration applies as long as secrecy holds.$q$),
('ci-q-063','cissp',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A company installs security cameras that record continuously but do not alert anyone in real time; footage is only reviewed after an incident is reported. Which functional control category does this represent?$q$,$q$["Detective","Preventive","Corrective","Deterrent"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A detective control identifies that an incident occurred (here, via recorded footage reviewed after the fact) - it does not stop the incident from happening (preventive) or fix the damage afterward (corrective).$q$),
('ci-q-064','cissp',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A document states "All laptops MUST be encrypted with full-disk encryption before deployment," while a separate document recommends "Consider using a password manager to generate unique passwords." What best describes these two documents, respectively?$q$,$q$["A mandatory policy/standard, and a discretionary guideline","Both are mandatory policies","Both are optional guidelines","A procedure and a law"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Mandatory, must-follow directives are policies/standards; recommended, discretionary best practices are guidelines - the "must" vs. "consider" language is the key distinguishing signal.$q$),
('ci-q-065','cissp',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which practice most directly reduces the effectiveness of phishing attacks over time by measuring and reinforcing actual employee behavior, rather than one-time compliance training?$q$,$q$["Recurring, unannounced phishing simulations with targeted follow-up training","A single annual slideshow with a quiz at the end","Firewall rule updates","Disabling employee email accounts"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Ongoing, unannounced simulated phishing exercises measure real behavior and let the organization target follow-up training where it is actually needed, which is more effective than one-time passive training.$q$),
('ci-q-066','cissp',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$Why should third-party vendor risk assessments continue periodically after a contract is signed, rather than occurring only during initial vendor selection?$q$,$q$["A vendor's security posture, subcontractors, and risk profile can change materially after onboarding","Vendor risk never changes once a contract is signed","Regulations prohibit reassessing a vendor after signing","Initial assessments are always sufficient forever"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Ownership changes, new subcontractors, breaches, or degraded controls can all occur after onboarding, so periodic reassessment - not a single point-in-time gate - is needed to manage ongoing third-party risk.$q$),
('ci-q-067','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$Under the GDPR, within how many hours of becoming aware of a qualifying personal data breach must a data controller generally notify the relevant supervisory authority?$q$,$q$["72 hours","24 hours","30 days","There is no notification requirement"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$GDPR Article 33 requires notifying the supervisory authority without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of becoming aware of the breach.$q$),
('ci-q-068','cissp',1,'1.2','mcq',3,$q$How does the PASTA threat modeling methodology primarily differ in focus from STRIDE?$q$,$q$["PASTA is a risk-centric methodology aligning threats to business impact and attacker motivation, while STRIDE is a developer-centric model categorizing threats by security property violated","PASTA and STRIDE are identical in every respect","STRIDE evaluates business risk while PASTA only looks at code","Neither model considers attacker behavior at all"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$PASTA (Process for Attack Simulation and Threat Analysis) is explicitly risk-centric, tying technical threats back to business impact, whereas STRIDE is a simpler categorization tool commonly applied during design/development.$q$),
('ci-q-069','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Who is ultimately accountable for classifying an organization's data and determining who is authorized to access it?$q$,$q$["The data owner","The data custodian","The end user","The system administrator"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The data owner (typically a business executive) is accountable for classification decisions and access authorization; custodians implement the day-to-day technical controls the owner directs.$q$),
('ci-q-070','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A company wants to redeploy old laptop hard drives to a different internal department at the same classification level, ensuring the previous department's ordinary files cannot be recovered using standard OS tools. Which NIST SP 800-88 sanitization category is appropriate?$q$,$q$["Clear","Purge","Destroy","No sanitization is needed"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Clear uses standard read/write commands (like overwriting) to sanitize media against simple, non-laboratory recovery methods - sufficient for reuse within the same organization at an equivalent sensitivity level.$q$),
('ci-q-071','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',3,$q$A company is donating a decommissioned hard drive that once stored top-secret data to an outside charity. Which sanitization approach provides the appropriate assurance level?$q$,$q$["Destroy (physical destruction of the media)","Clear (a single overwrite pass)","No action, since the drive is old","Purge is unnecessary if the drive still functions"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$When media leaves organizational control entirely and previously held the most sensitive classification, physical destruction provides the highest assurance against any recovery, including advanced laboratory techniques.$q$),
('ci-q-072','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A cloud provider offers hardware-based memory encryption so that even the cloud provider's own privileged administrators cannot inspect a customer's data while it is actively being processed by the CPU. Which data state does this protect?$q$,$q$["Data in use","Data at rest","Data in transit","Data at destruction"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Data in use refers to data actively being processed in memory/CPU - confidential computing techniques like memory encryption specifically protect this state, distinct from at-rest (storage) or in-transit (network) protections.$q$),
('ci-q-073','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A team selects only the NIST SP 800-53 control families applicable to a system categorized as low-impact under FIPS 199, excluding controls only relevant to moderate/high-impact systems. What is this activity called?$q$,$q$["Scoping","Tailoring","Data remanence analysis","Sanitization"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Scoping is the process of determining which controls from a baseline actually apply to a given system based on its characteristics (like impact level), before any further customization occurs.$q$),
('ci-q-074','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$After selecting an applicable security control baseline, an organization adjusts a specific control's parameter (e.g., changing minimum password length from the baseline default) to match its own risk tolerance and operational environment. What is this activity called?$q$,$q$["Tailoring","Scoping","Data classification","Media sanitization"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Tailoring customizes already-selected controls to fit an organization's specific environment and risk appetite, whereas scoping determines which controls apply in the first place.$q$),
('ci-q-075','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Under GDPR, which role is defined as the entity that determines the purposes and means of processing personal data?$q$,$q$["Data controller","Data processor","Data subject","Data custodian"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The data controller decides why and how personal data is processed; a data processor acts only on the controller's instructions, and the data subject is the individual the data is about.$q$),
('ci-q-076','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$An organization retains detailed customer purchase records for eight years after its documented seven-year regulatory retention requirement expired, with no defined business justification. What risk does this practice primarily increase?$q$,$q$["Breach liability and legal exposure from retaining data longer than necessary","Improved customer service quality with no downside","Reduced storage costs","Guaranteed regulatory compliance"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Retaining data beyond its required/justified retention period increases the amount of sensitive data exposed in a breach and creates legal exposure, since the data serves no ongoing lawful purpose.$q$),
('ci-q-077','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Which classification level is appropriate for information whose unauthorized disclosure could reasonably be expected to cause exceptionally grave damage to national security?$q$,$q$["Top Secret","Confidential","Secret","Unclassified"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$In the U.S. government classification scheme, Top Secret is reserved for information causing "exceptionally grave damage," Secret for "serious damage," and Confidential for "damage" - a defined escalating scale.$q$),
('ci-q-078','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A multi-level secure system must ensure a user cleared only for Secret cannot read documents classified Top Secret, while still permitting that user to write information upward into a Top Secret file. Which security model's simple security property enforces this behavior?$q$,$q$["Bell-LaPadula","Biba","Clark-Wilson","Brewer-Nash"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Bell-LaPadula's simple security property ("no read up") is designed exactly for confidentiality-focused multi-level systems, blocking reads of higher classifications while its star property allows writing to higher levels.$q$),
('ci-q-079','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A system must prevent a low-integrity, untrusted process from modifying a high-integrity configuration file, even though the untrusted process is permitted to read that file. Which security model's star-integrity axiom enforces this?$q$,$q$["Biba","Bell-LaPadula","Brewer-Nash","Clark-Wilson"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Biba's star-integrity axiom ("no write up") prevents lower-integrity subjects from writing to higher-integrity objects, protecting data integrity rather than confidentiality.$q$),
('ci-q-080','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A bank's core system requires that all account balance changes occur only through a vetted, audited application, never via direct database edits by any user, and separates the roles of transaction initiator and approver. Which model does this design most directly implement?$q$,$q$["Clark-Wilson","Bell-LaPadula","Biba","Brewer-Nash"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Clark-Wilson enforces integrity through well-formed transactions (access only via a trusted program) and separation of duties (initiator vs. approver) - exactly this design pattern.$q$),
('ci-q-081','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A consulting firm's analyst who has accessed Company A's confidential merger data must be automatically blocked from later accessing a direct competitor Company B's data in the same conflict-of-interest class. Which model enforces this dynamic restriction?$q$,$q$["Brewer-Nash (Chinese Wall)","Bell-LaPadula","Biba","Clark-Wilson"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Brewer-Nash model dynamically restricts access based on a subject's prior access history, specifically to prevent conflict-of-interest exposure between competing clients in the same class.$q$),
('ci-q-082','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A product is Common Criteria evaluated at EAL4, and a competing product is evaluated at EAL2. What does the higher EAL rating alone confirm?$q$,$q$["The EAL4 product underwent a more rigorous and thorough evaluation and testing process, not necessarily that it is inherently more secure in practice","The EAL4 product is guaranteed to have zero vulnerabilities","The EAL2 product cannot be used in any regulated environment","EAL ratings measure encryption key length only"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Evaluation Assurance Levels measure the rigor and depth of the assurance/testing process applied during evaluation, not a guarantee of real-world security outcomes or the absence of vulnerabilities.$q$),
('ci-q-083','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$Which set of three properties must a reference monitor satisfy?$q$,$q$["Always invoked (complete mediation), tamperproof, and small enough to be verifiable","Fast, cheap, and open source","Encrypted, redundant, and cloud-hosted","Owned by a third party, audited annually, and physically air-gapped"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The reference monitor concept requires complete mediation of every access, resistance to tampering, and a small enough implementation to be formally verifiable - these three properties are foundational to trusted system design.$q$),
('ci-q-084','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$A VPN gateway needs to encrypt a high volume of bulk data traffic with minimal performance overhead. Which type of cryptography is best suited to this specific task?$q$,$q$["Symmetric key cryptography","Asymmetric key cryptography alone","Hashing alone","Steganography"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Symmetric algorithms are computationally efficient for bulk data encryption, which is why most real-world systems use asymmetric cryptography only to exchange the symmetric session key, then switch to symmetric encryption for the actual data.$q$),
('ci-q-085','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$A sender wants a recipient to be able to verify both that a message was not altered and that only the sender could have produced it. Which process achieves this?$q$,$q$["Hash the message, then encrypt the hash with the sender's private key (a digital signature)","Encrypt the entire message with the recipient's private key","Hash the message with no encryption","Encrypt the message with a shared symmetric key only"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A digital signature hashes the message for integrity, then encrypts that hash with the sender's private key; only the sender's paired public key can successfully verify it, providing both integrity and non-repudiation.$q$),
('ci-q-086','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',3,$q$An attacker who possesses only intercepted encrypted messages, with no corresponding plaintext, attempts to break the cipher. What type of cryptanalysis attack is this?$q$,$q$["Ciphertext-only attack","Known-plaintext attack","Chosen-plaintext attack","Chosen-ciphertext attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A ciphertext-only attack relies solely on captured ciphertext with no known plaintext pairs, making it the most difficult attack type to succeed with, compared to known/chosen-plaintext attacks that assume more attacker knowledge.$q$),
('ci-q-087','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$A facility designs its parking lot lighting, low landscaping, and building entrances so that employees can naturally observe activity around the property from normal vantage points. Which physical security concept does this apply?$q$,$q$["Natural surveillance (a CPTED principle)","Faraday caging","Mantrap design","TEMPEST shielding"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Natural surveillance is a core Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design (CPTED) principle - designing spaces so legitimate occupants can naturally observe their surroundings, discouraging illegitimate activity.$q$),
('ci-q-088','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$Why do many industrial control system (ICS/SCADA) environments prioritize availability over confidentiality when weighing security trade-offs?$q$,$q$["Disrupting a physical process (e.g., halting a power grid or water treatment system) can cause immediate safety and operational harm, making uptime the paramount concern","Confidentiality is never relevant in any ICS environment","ICS systems have no legacy or patching constraints","Availability is only a concern for standard IT systems"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$In ICS/SCADA environments, an outage can directly endanger physical safety or critical infrastructure operation, which is why availability is typically weighted above confidentiality relative to standard IT priorities.$q$),
('ci-q-089','cissp',3,'3.5','mcq',2,$q$An electronic door lock loses power during a fire. For life-safety reasons, the door automatically unlocks so occupants can evacuate. What design principle does this behavior reflect?$q$,$q$["Fail-safe (fail-open) - prioritizing human safety over access restriction during a failure","Fail-secure - the door should remain locked during any failure","Least privilege","Separation of duties"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Fail-safe (fail-open) designs prioritize life safety, unlocking during a power failure so people can evacuate - the opposite of fail-secure, which would keep sensitive areas locked during a failure at the cost of egress.$q$),
('ci-q-090','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Which OSI layer is responsible for logical addressing and routing packets between different networks?$q$,$q$["Layer 3 (Network)","Layer 2 (Data Link)","Layer 4 (Transport)","Layer 7 (Application)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Network layer (Layer 3) handles logical addressing (IP) and routing decisions between networks, distinct from the Data Link layer's local, physical-address-based framing.$q$),
('ci-q-091','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Which OSI layer is responsible for reliable, ordered delivery of data, including retransmission of lost segments (as TCP does)?$q$,$q$["Layer 4 (Transport)","Layer 3 (Network)","Layer 2 (Data Link)","Layer 5 (Session)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Transport layer (Layer 4) provides end-to-end reliability, sequencing, and retransmission for connection-oriented protocols like TCP.$q$),
('ci-q-092','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Two branch offices establish a site-to-site VPN that encrypts and encapsulates the entire original IP packet, including its header, inside a new IP packet for transit across the internet. Which IPsec mode is this?$q$,$q$["Tunnel mode","Transport mode","Quick mode","Aggressive mode"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Tunnel mode encrypts and encapsulates the entire original packet (including its header) inside a new packet - the standard choice for gateway-to-gateway VPNs, as opposed to transport mode's host-to-host, payload-only protection.$q$),
('ci-q-093','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Which IPsec protocol provides confidentiality (encryption) of the payload, in addition to integrity and authentication?$q$,$q$["ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload)","AH (Authentication Header) alone","ICMP","ARP"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$ESP provides encryption plus integrity/authentication; AH provides only integrity and authentication with no encryption at all - a key distinction tested on the exam.$q$),
('ci-q-094','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A firewall tracks the state of active connections and only permits inbound packets that correspond to an already-established outbound session, rather than evaluating each packet in isolation. What type of firewall is this?$q$,$q$["Stateful inspection firewall","Static packet-filtering firewall","Proxy firewall only","A hub"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Stateful inspection firewalls maintain a state table of active connections, allowing them to make context-aware decisions that a simple stateless packet filter (evaluating each packet independently) cannot.$q$),
('ci-q-095','cissp',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$A detection system identifies traffic matching a known exploit's exact byte pattern from a maintained database. Which detection method is this?$q$,$q$["Signature-based detection","Anomaly-based detection","Heuristic behavioral baselining","Reputation-based detection only"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Signature-based detection matches traffic against a database of known attack patterns - effective against known threats but blind to novel attacks, unlike anomaly-based detection which flags deviations from a learned baseline.$q$),
('ci-q-096','cissp',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$A security appliance sits inline with network traffic and can automatically drop malicious packets in real time before they reach their destination. What type of system is this, as opposed to one that only alerts after the fact?$q$,$q$["An Intrusion Prevention System (IPS)","An Intrusion Detection System (IDS) only","A honeypot","A vulnerability scanner"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An IPS sits inline and can actively block traffic in real time; an IDS is typically deployed out-of-band and can only alert, not prevent, since it is not in the direct traffic path.$q$),
('ci-q-097','cissp',4,'4.5','mcq',3,$q$WPA3 replaces WPA2's four-way handshake with Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE). What weakness does this primarily address?$q$,$q$["Susceptibility to offline dictionary/brute-force attacks against a captured handshake, as exploited by attacks like KRACK","SAE makes the network faster but adds no security benefit","WPA2 handshakes were already immune to offline attacks","SAE removes the need for any password entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SAE is designed to resist offline dictionary attacks against a captured handshake exchange, closing a class of weaknesses in WPA2's four-way handshake that attacks like KRACK exploited.$q$),
('ci-q-098','cissp',4,'4.6','mcq',2,$q$An attacker captures unencrypted RTP traffic on a shared network segment and reconstructs a private VoIP phone conversation. What control would have prevented this?$q$,$q$["Using SRTP (Secure RTP) to encrypt the media stream","Disabling DNS","Using a longer phone extension number","Removing the phone's power supply"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SRTP encrypts the actual voice media stream, preventing eavesdropping on captured RTP traffic - plain RTP carries voice data in the clear unless SRTP or an equivalent protection is used.$q$),
('ci-q-099','cissp',4,'4.7','mcq',2,$q$A company places IoT devices, guest Wi-Fi users, and finance servers on separate VLANs with restrictive inter-VLAN routing rules. What is the primary security benefit of this design?$q$,$q$["Limiting lateral movement and blast radius if any one segment is compromised","Eliminating the need for any firewall","Guaranteeing zero latency between segments","Making DHCP unnecessary"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Network segmentation confines a compromise to the segment where it occurred, restricting an attacker's ability to pivot laterally toward more sensitive segments like finance servers.$q$),
('ci-q-100','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$An administrator disables Telnet access to network devices in favor of SSH for all remote management. What is the primary security justification?$q$,$q$["SSH encrypts the session, while Telnet transmits credentials and data in plaintext","Telnet is faster than SSH","SSH does not require authentication","Telnet supports more simultaneous sessions"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SSH encrypts the entire session including credentials, closing off the plaintext credential-sniffing risk inherent to Telnet.$q$),
('ci-q-101','cissp',4,'4.7','mcq',3,$q$A company runs iSCSI storage traffic on the same flat, unsegmented network as general user data traffic, with no additional isolation. What risk does this converged-protocol design primarily introduce?$q$,$q$["Storage traffic becomes exposed to interception or interference by any compromised host on the same broad network segment","iSCSI cannot function on a shared network under any configuration","This design has no additional risk versus a dedicated storage network","iSCSI automatically encrypts itself regardless of network design"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Converging storage protocols like iSCSI onto a general-purpose, unsegmented network exposes storage traffic to any compromised host on that same broadcast/network domain, unlike a properly isolated storage network.$q$),
('ci-q-102','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',3,$q$A Software-Defined Networking (SDN) architecture centralizes network-wide forwarding decisions in a controller, separate from the individual switches that only forward traffic based on the controller's instructions. What architectural principle does this reflect?$q$,$q$["Separation of the control plane from the data plane","Combining the control and data planes into every switch","Eliminating the need for any network policy","Replacing all physical switches with firewalls"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SDN's defining characteristic is separating the control plane (decision-making, centralized in the controller) from the data plane (packet forwarding, distributed across switches).$q$),
('ci-q-103','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$A remote employee's VPN client is configured so that only traffic destined for the corporate network passes through the VPN tunnel, while all other internet traffic goes directly out the employee's local connection, bypassing corporate security inspection. What is this configuration called, and what risk does it introduce?$q$,$q$["Split tunneling, which can let unfiltered internet traffic bypass corporate security controls","Full tunneling, which routes everything through the VPN with no risk","A site-to-site VPN, which does not apply to individual users","Static NAT, which duplicates internet traffic for inspection"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Split tunneling improves performance and reduces VPN load, but the non-corporate traffic entirely bypasses corporate proxies, filtering, and monitoring - a well-known security trade-off.$q$),
('ci-q-104','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$A user swipes an access badge at a door reader, then is prompted to enter a PIN which the system verifies against the badge's associated record before unlocking. Which step does the PIN verification represent in the IAAA model?$q$,$q$["Authentication","Identification","Authorization","Accountability"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Identification is the badge swipe (claiming an identity); authentication is proving that claim is genuine, which the verified PIN accomplishes here.$q$),
('ci-q-105','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$An organization can trace every file deletion in a sensitive system back to the specific authenticated user who performed it, via unique user IDs in the audit log. Which IAAA component does this capability represent?$q$,$q$["Accountability","Identification","Authorization","Authentication"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Accountability is the ability to trace actions back to a specific individual, which requires unique identities and reliable logging - shared accounts would break this property entirely.$q$),
('ci-q-106','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$A file's owner personally decides which specific colleagues may read or edit that file, and can grant or revoke that access at any time at their own discretion. Which access control model does this represent?$q$,$q$["Discretionary Access Control (DAC)","Mandatory Access Control (MAC)","Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)","Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DAC lets the resource owner control access at their own discretion - the defining trait distinguishing it from MAC's system-enforced, non-discretionary labeling.$q$),
('ci-q-107','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$In a classified government system, a user's access to a document is determined entirely by comparing the user's clearance level to the document's classification label, and the user has no ability to alter this regardless of their own preference. Which model is this?$q$,$q$["Mandatory Access Control (MAC)","Discretionary Access Control (DAC)","Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)","Rule-based access control only"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$MAC enforces access based on system-assigned labels and clearances that individual users cannot override - a hallmark of high-assurance, classified environments.$q$),
('ci-q-108','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$A company assigns permissions based on job function, so that everyone hired into the "Accountant" role automatically receives the same predefined set of system permissions. Which access control model is this?$q$,$q$["Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)","Discretionary Access Control (DAC)","Mandatory Access Control (MAC)","Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RBAC ties permissions to a defined role rather than to the individual or a resource owner's discretion, simplifying administration for common job functions.$q$),
('ci-q-109','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',3,$q$A policy grants access to a sensitive application only if the request comes from a company-managed device, during business hours, AND from a user in the finance department - evaluating multiple independent conditions together at request time. Which access control model is this?$q$,$q$["Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)","Discretionary Access Control (DAC)","Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) alone","Mandatory Access Control (MAC) alone"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$ABAC evaluates multiple attributes (device ownership, time, department) dynamically at request time, offering more granular, context-aware control than role membership alone.$q$),
('ci-q-110','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$In a SAML-based web SSO flow, which two parties exchange a signed assertion confirming the user has already been authenticated?$q$,$q$["The Identity Provider (IdP) and the Service Provider (SP)","The user's browser and their local ISP","Two Service Providers directly, with no IdP involved","The KDC and the ticket-granting service"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The IdP authenticates the user and issues a signed SAML assertion that the SP trusts to grant access, without the SP needing to independently verify the user's credentials.$q$),
('ci-q-111','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$A user grants a third-party photo-printing app limited access to view their cloud photo album, without ever sharing their cloud account password with that app. Which protocol is designed for this delegated-authorization scenario?$q$,$q$["OAuth 2.0","Kerberos","RADIUS","LDAP"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$OAuth 2.0 is purpose-built for delegated authorization, issuing a scoped access token to the third party without exposing the user's actual credentials.$q$),
('ci-q-112','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',3,$q$Users at a branch office suddenly cannot authenticate to a Kerberos-protected domain after a workstation's clock drifts more than five minutes out of sync with the Key Distribution Center. What does this scenario illustrate?$q$,$q$["Kerberos authentication depends on tightly synchronized clocks to prevent replay and validate ticket timestamps","Kerberos is unaffected by system clock settings","This indicates a firewall misconfiguration unrelated to Kerberos","Kerberos does not use time-based tickets at all"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Kerberos tickets are time-stamped and typically rejected outside a small allowed clock skew (often 5 minutes) specifically to prevent replay attacks - making time synchronization a core operational dependency.$q$),
('ci-q-113','cissp',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$A login process requires a fingerprint scan and a physical hardware token. Which two authentication factor categories does this combination use?$q$,$q$["Something you are, and something you have","Something you know, and something you know","Something you have, and something you have","Somewhere you are, and something you know"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A fingerprint is "something you are" (biometric); a hardware token is "something you have" - combining two distinct factor categories is what makes this true multi-factor authentication.$q$),
('ci-q-114','cissp',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$An employee is terminated but their account credentials remain active in several systems for three weeks afterward due to a manual, inconsistent offboarding process. What risk does this delay primarily create?$q$,$q$["An extended window in which a disgruntled former employee (or anyone with their credentials) could access systems without authorization","No risk, since the employee no longer has a badge","Improved security, since the account is well-documented","A compliance benefit under most frameworks"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Timely deprovisioning is a core IAM control - delayed account disablement after termination creates an avoidable window for insider or credential-based misuse.$q$),
('ci-q-115','cissp',5,'5.7','mcq',3,$q$An attacker who compromises a Windows workstation captures a cached NTLM password hash and uses it directly to authenticate to other systems on the network, without ever cracking the underlying plaintext password. What is this attack called?$q$,$q$["Pass-the-hash","Credential stuffing","Rainbow table attack","Kerberoasting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Pass-the-hash reuses the captured hash itself for authentication (since many protocols accept the hash as a credential), skipping the need to ever recover the original plaintext password.$q$),
('ci-q-116','cissp',5,'5.7','mcq',2,$q$An attacker takes username/password pairs leaked from a breach of one website and successfully logs into many unrelated websites where the same users reused those credentials. What is this attack called?$q$,$q$["Credential stuffing","Pass-the-hash","A brute-force attack against a single account","A man-in-the-middle attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Credential stuffing exploits password reuse across sites using previously breached credential pairs, succeeding at scale wherever users reused the same password.$q$),
('ci-q-117','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$A penetration tester is given no internal documentation, network diagrams, or credentials, and must attempt to compromise the target exactly as an external attacker would. What type of test is this?$q$,$q$["Black box test","White box test","Gray box test","A vulnerability scan only"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Black box testing simulates a real external attacker's limited starting knowledge, providing the most realistic (but potentially least comprehensive) view of exploitable exposure.$q$),
('ci-q-118','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$A penetration tester is given full source code, network architecture diagrams, and administrative documentation before testing begins. What type of test is this, and what is its main advantage?$q$,$q$["White box test - it provides the most thorough test coverage since the tester can target areas an external attacker might never discover","Black box test - it simulates a real attacker","Gray box test - it uses only user-level credentials","This is not a form of penetration testing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$White box testing's full-knowledge approach allows the deepest, most comprehensive coverage, since the tester is not limited to only what could be discovered from the outside.$q$),
('ci-q-119','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$A penetration tester is given a standard employee's login credentials but no source code or architecture diagrams, and is asked to test what an authenticated insider could accomplish. What type of test is this?$q$,$q$["Gray box test","Black box test","White box test","A tabletop exercise only"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Gray box testing provides partial knowledge (here, standard user access) - a middle ground between the zero-knowledge black box and full-knowledge white box approaches.$q$),
('ci-q-120','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$An automated vulnerability scan flags a missing security patch on a server, but the report alone does not confirm whether the vulnerability is actually exploitable in this environment. What activity would be needed to prove real-world impact?$q$,$q$["A penetration test that actively attempts to exploit the finding","Re-running the same vulnerability scan again","Ignoring the finding since scans are always accurate","Updating the asset inventory only"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Vulnerability assessments identify and catalog potential weaknesses, but only active exploitation attempts (penetration testing) confirm whether a finding is genuinely exploitable in context.$q$),
('ci-q-121','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',3,$q$An auditor reviews a firewall's written configuration standard document, then separately asks the firewall administrator to describe how they would respond to a detected intrusion, and finally attempts to actually trigger and observe the intrusion detection alert. Which three NIST SP 800-53A assessment methods does this sequence represent, respectively?$q$,$q$["Examine, Interview, Test","Test, Examine, Interview","Interview, Test, Examine","Examine, Test, Interview"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Examine reviews documentation/artifacts, Interview gathers information from personnel, and Test directly exercises the mechanism - the auditor performed exactly this sequence in that order.$q$),
('ci-q-122','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',2,$q$A prospective enterprise customer requires evidence that a SaaS vendor's security controls operated effectively over the preceding six months, not just that they were well-designed at a single point in time. Which report should the vendor provide?$q$,$q$["A SOC 2 Type II report","A SOC 2 Type I report","A Common Criteria certificate","A penetration test summary alone"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SOC 2 Type II evaluates operating effectiveness over a period (commonly 6-12 months); Type I only assesses whether controls were suitably designed at one point in time.$q$),
('ci-q-123','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',2,$q$An organization runs an automated script every 15 minutes that completes a simulated end-to-end purchase on its e-commerce site, verifying real functionality and confirming monitoring alerts would fire if it failed. What is this technique called?$q$,$q$["A synthetic transaction","A tabletop exercise","A static code review","A vulnerability scan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Synthetic transactions simulate real user activity on a schedule to proactively verify functionality and monitoring/alerting, rather than waiting for a real user to report a failure.$q$),
('ci-q-124','cissp',6,'6.6','mcq',2,$q$A team scans uncompiled application source code for patterns indicative of SQL injection before the code is ever deployed or run. What type of testing is this?$q$,$q$["Static Application Security Testing (SAST)","Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST)","A penetration test","A synthetic transaction"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SAST analyzes source code without executing it, catching certain classes of flaws earlier in the SDLC; DAST instead tests a running application with live inputs.$q$),
('ci-q-125','cissp',6,'6.7','mcq',2,$q$A security dashboard shows the monthly count of failed login attempts steadily trending upward over the past six months, serving as an early warning of increasing account-compromise risk before any breach occurs. What type of metric is this?$q$,$q$["A Key Risk Indicator (KRI)","A Key Performance Indicator (KPI) only","A Service Level Agreement (SLA)","A Recovery Point Objective (RPO)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A KRI provides forward-looking early warning of rising risk exposure, distinct from a KPI, which measures how well an existing process is performing against a target.$q$),
('ci-q-126','cissp',6,'6.7','mcq',2,$q$A security team reports that its average time to patch critical vulnerabilities dropped from 30 days to 10 days this quarter. What type of metric is this?$q$,$q$["A Key Performance Indicator (KPI)","A Key Risk Indicator (KRI) only","A Recovery Time Objective (RTO)","An Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This measures how effectively an existing process (patch management) is performing against a goal, which is the defining characteristic of a KPI, as opposed to a KRI's forward-looking risk warning.$q$),
('ci-q-127','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',2,$q$Before signing a contract with a SaaS vendor, a company's security team requests an independent third-party report describing the vendor's control environment and testing results, rather than only relying on the vendor's own self-attestation. What are they most likely requesting?$q$,$q$["A SOC 2 report","A patent filing","A trademark registration","A BIA from the customer's own organization"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A SOC 2 report is an independent auditor's evaluation of a vendor's controls, giving prospective customers third-party assurance beyond the vendor's own claims.$q$),
('ci-q-128','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',2,$q$A security analyst manually reviewing authentication logs notices repeated failed login attempts against an administrator account at 3 AM from a country the organization has no business presence in. What security assessment activity uncovered this?$q$,$q$["Log review","A penetration test","A tabletop exercise","Code review"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Proactive, manual log review can surface anomalies like unusual timing and geography that automated alerting thresholds might miss, complementing automated monitoring.$q$),
('ci-q-129','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$Why do mature security programs typically combine automated vulnerability scanning with periodic manual penetration testing, rather than relying on either alone?$q$,$q$["Automated scanning provides broad, frequent coverage of known issues, while manual testing finds business-logic flaws and chains of exploitation that automated tools typically miss","Automated scanning always finds every vulnerability, making manual testing redundant","Manual testing is always faster and cheaper than automated scanning","Combining both approaches provides no additional value over either one alone"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Automated scanning excels at broad, repeatable coverage of known signatures, while skilled manual testers can identify complex business-logic flaws and multi-step attack chains that automated tools are not designed to detect.$q$),
('ci-q-130','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',2,$q$An investigator collects a hard drive as evidence but fails to document who handled it, when, and for what purpose during transport to a lab. What is the most significant consequence of this failure?$q$,$q$["The evidence may be ruled inadmissible in court due to a broken chain of custody","The evidence automatically becomes more credible","No consequence, as long as the drive itself is unmodified","The investigator is automatically immune from cross-examination"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An unbroken, documented chain of custody is required to establish that evidence was not tampered with; gaps in that documented trail are a common basis for evidence being challenged or excluded in legal proceedings.$q$),
('ci-q-131','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',2,$q$During an active malware outbreak, a response team disconnects an infected workstation from the network to stop it from spreading further, before performing any deeper analysis. Under the (ISC)² incident management phases, which phase does this action represent?$q$,$q$["Mitigation","Detection","Reporting","Lessons Learned"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Mitigation focuses on limiting the scope and impact of an ongoing incident (such as isolating an infected host) - it occurs after detection but before full recovery and remediation activities.$q$),
('ci-q-132','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',2,$q$A SIEM correlation rule fires an alert after observing an unusual spike in outbound traffic from a single workstation at 2 AM. Which incident management phase does this alert represent?$q$,$q$["Detection","Mitigation","Recovery","Remediation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Detection is the phase of identifying that a potential incident is occurring in the first place - here, via an automated SIEM alert - before any response action has yet been taken.$q$),
('ci-q-133','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',2,$q$A proposed production database schema change must be reviewed and formally approved by a Change Advisory Board before implementation, following a documented request, review, and approval workflow. What process does this describe?$q$,$q$["Change management","Configuration management","Patch management","Incident management"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Change management governs the structured request-review-approve-implement workflow for intentional modifications to production systems, typically overseen by a CAB.$q$),
('ci-q-134','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',2,$q$An organization maintains a documented baseline of approved software versions across its server fleet and automatically flags any server that drifts from that approved baseline. What process does this describe?$q$,$q$["Configuration management","Change management","Patch management","Risk management"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Configuration management establishes and enforces a known-good baseline state, detecting unauthorized drift - complementary to, but distinct from, the approval workflow of change management.$q$),
('ci-q-135','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',3,$q$In response to a newly disclosed zero-day vulnerability, a team first deploys the emergency vendor patch to a lab environment mirroring production, validates it does not break critical functionality, and only then deploys it to production. What best practice does this reflect?$q$,$q$["Testing patches in a representative environment before production deployment, even under time pressure","Deploying every patch directly to production immediately regardless of testing","Skipping patch management entirely for zero-day fixes","Only patching systems that have already been compromised"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Even urgent, high-priority patches should be validated in a representative test environment first where feasible, since an untested patch can itself cause outages - one of the core tensions in patch management.$q$),
('ci-q-136','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',2,$q$A BCP test consists solely of gathering stakeholders in a room to walk through the plan's steps verbally, without touching any live system. Which type of test is this, and where does it rank in terms of risk and cost?$q$,$q$["A tabletop exercise - the lowest-risk, lowest-cost type of BCP/DRP test","A full interruption test - the highest-risk type","A parallel test - moderate risk","A simulation test - moderate-to-high risk"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Tabletop exercises are discussion-based walkthroughs with no live systems touched, making them the safest and least expensive way to validate a plan, though also the least realistic.$q$),
('ci-q-137','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',3,$q$A DR test actually fails over live production operations to the disaster recovery site, temporarily disrupting real business operations, to fully validate the recovery process end to end. Which type of test is this, and what is its primary trade-off?$q$,$q$["A full interruption test - the most realistic validation, but also the highest risk and cost","A tabletop exercise - low risk and low realism","A checklist review - no live systems involved","A structured walkthrough - moderate realism only"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A full interruption test provides the most realistic proof that the DR plan actually works, at the cost of genuinely disrupting live operations - the highest-risk, highest-cost test type on the spectrum.$q$),
('ci-q-138','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',2,$q$Which RAID level provides redundancy by writing identical copies of data to two drives simultaneously, tolerating the complete loss of one drive with no parity calculation involved?$q$,$q$["RAID 1 (mirroring)","RAID 0 (striping)","RAID 5 (striping with parity)","RAID 6 (dual parity)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RAID 1 mirrors data across drives with no parity math, providing straightforward redundancy at the cost of storage efficiency (only 50% usable capacity for two drives).$q$),
('ci-q-139','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',2,$q$Which RAID level stripes data across multiple drives with distributed parity information, allowing the array to survive the failure of any single drive?$q$,$q$["RAID 5","RAID 0","RAID 1","JBOD"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RAID 5 distributes both data and parity information across all drives in the array, allowing reconstruction of data after a single drive failure, unlike RAID 0 which has no redundancy at all.$q$),
('ci-q-140','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',2,$q$A secure facility uses a small enclosed space with two interlocking doors, where the second door only unlocks after the first has closed and the occupant is verified, allowing only one person through at a time. What physical control is this, and what does it prevent?$q$,$q$["A mantrap, preventing tailgating/piggybacking into the secure area","A Faraday cage, blocking electromagnetic signals","A bollard, stopping vehicle-based attacks","A demilitarized zone, isolating network segments"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A mantrap's interlocking-door design physically prevents more than one person from passing through per authentication event, directly addressing tailgating/piggybacking risk.$q$),
('ci-q-141','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',2,$q$An employee attempts to email a spreadsheet containing customer credit card numbers to their personal email address, and the action is automatically blocked and logged by a monitoring tool inspecting outbound traffic. What type of control performed this action?$q$,$q$["Data Loss Prevention (DLP) / egress monitoring","An intrusion detection system","A vulnerability scanner","A configuration management tool"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DLP tools inspect outbound (egress) traffic for sensitive data patterns and can block or quarantine transmissions that violate policy - exactly the scenario described.$q$),
('ci-q-142','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',2,$q$After an incident is fully contained and systems are recovered, the response team documents the root cause, evaluates what worked and what did not, and updates the incident response playbook accordingly. Which incident management phase does this represent?$q$,$q$["Lessons Learned","Detection","Mitigation","Reporting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Lessons Learned is the final phase, focused on improving future response through root-cause documentation and process updates - distinct from the earlier detection, mitigation, and reporting phases.$q$),
('ci-q-143','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',2,$q$A development team integrates automated static code analysis (SAST) directly into the CI pipeline so that vulnerabilities are flagged at every commit, rather than only during a final security review before release. What principle does this reflect?$q$,$q$["Shift-left security - addressing security earlier in the SDLC to reduce the cost and impact of fixing issues","Shift-right security - testing only occurs in production","Waterfall development, which forbids any automated testing","Zero trust networking"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Shift-left security moves testing and review earlier in the development lifecycle, since defects found and fixed at commit time are dramatically cheaper than those found after release.$q$),
('ci-q-144','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',2,$q$A secure development policy requires all engineers to be trained on the OWASP Top 10. What is the primary purpose of referencing this list in the policy?$q$,$q$["It provides a standardized, widely recognized awareness baseline of the most critical and common web application security risks","It is a legally binding international law","It replaces the need for any code review","It only applies to mobile applications, not web applications"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The OWASP Top 10 is an industry-standard awareness document listing the most critical web application security risks, used broadly as a training and prioritization baseline rather than a legal mandate.$q$),
('ci-q-145','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',3,$q$An analyst combines several individually unclassified intelligence reports and, by correlating them, deduces a classified troop movement that no single report revealed on its own. What type of attack does this represent?$q$,$q$["Aggregation","Inference","SQL injection","Cross-site scripting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Aggregation occurs when combining multiple pieces of individually low-sensitivity information reveals something more sensitive than any single piece alone - the defining trait distinguishing it from single-source inference.$q$),
('ci-q-146','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',3,$q$A user without direct access to an employee salary field deduces that employee's approximate salary range by correlating accessible bonus amounts and tax bracket data. What type of attack does this represent?$q$,$q$["Inference","Aggregation","Buffer overflow","Cross-site request forgery"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Inference involves deducing restricted information from accessible related data through reasoning, distinct from aggregation, which combines multiple separate low-sensitivity data points directly.$q$),
('ci-q-147','cissp',8,'8.4','mcq',2,$q$A public-facing API endpoint has no limit on how many requests a single client can make per minute, and an automated script floods it with requests, degrading service for all users. What security control was missing?$q$,$q$["Rate limiting","Input validation","TLS encryption","A firewall rule"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Rate limiting caps the number of requests a client can make in a given period, directly mitigating abusive or automated request floods against an API - a missing control here.$q$),
('ci-q-148','cissp',8,'8.5','mcq',2,$q$A code repository's mandatory peer-review policy catches and blocks a pull request containing a hardcoded cloud API key before it can be merged into the main branch. What benefit does this illustrate?$q$,$q$["Code review and secrets scanning practices prevent sensitive credentials from being committed into shared source control","Peer review only checks code style, never security issues","Hardcoded API keys are always safe to commit","This benefit only applies to open-source repositories"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Mandatory review (often paired with automated secrets scanning) is a key secure-repository practice that catches accidental credential leaks before they are permanently committed to version history.$q$),
('ci-q-149','cissp',8,'8.6','mcq',2,$q$A piece of malware spreads automatically across a network by exploiting a vulnerable service, requiring no user to open a file or click anything, and does not need to attach itself to an existing host file. What type of malware is this?$q$,$q$["A worm","A virus","A trojan","Ransomware, specifically"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Worms self-propagate across networks autonomously, without requiring user interaction or attachment to a host file - unlike a virus, which needs a host file and typically some user action to spread.$q$),
('ci-q-150','cissp',8,'8.6','mcq',2,$q$A user downloads what appears to be a legitimate free video-conversion utility, but the installer secretly also installs a hidden backdoor granting an attacker remote access. What type of malware is this?$q$,$q$["A trojan","A worm","A boot sector virus","A logic bomb"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A trojan disguises malicious functionality inside seemingly legitimate software, tricking the user into voluntarily installing it - distinct from a worm's autonomous network propagation.$q$);
